From 477f0550fb93b7693d6ed0eeee80510a74f27510 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Simone Salerno <doppiaesse@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 12:26:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] meta/redis: serve reads from a local read replica on
 read-write mounts

Upstream JuiceFS reads from Redis replicas only on read-only mounts, through
Sentinel or Cluster and `route-read=replica`. With the metadata on a master
across a WAN (10-30 ms), every lookup, getattr, getxattr and readdir costs a
round trip, and an SMB server in front of the mount issues thousands of them
per folder listing, and every write costs WATCH, the reads, MULTI/EXEC and
UNWATCH. This patch lets a read-write mount read from a replica of the
master, usually on the same machine, keeping read-your-writes, and commits
transactions in one round trip.

Configuration, in the metadata URL of a single Redis/Valkey master:

  redis://:PASSWORD@master:6379/1?read-replica=redis://:PASSWORD@127.0.0.1:6380/1&max-lag=2s&master-timeout=10s

- read-replica: URL of a replica of that master. Without a password it uses
  the master's (also META_PASSWORD); without a database it uses the master's,
  and a different database is an error. Reserved characters in its password
  must be percent-encoded twice, or the password left out.
- max-lag (default 2s): the oldest data the replica may serve while the
  master is reachable.
- master-timeout (default 10s): how long the master may stay silent (not a
  byte on any connection) before the client goes offline. A command waits
  for the master up to twice that (go-redis ContextTimeoutEnabled plus a
  deadline in a hook, retries included) and then fails with the usual error.
- Errors: Sentinel or Cluster masters, client-cache together with
  read-replica, max-lag or master-timeout <= 0. Without read-replica nothing
  changes except that a session cleanup also deletes its (missing) ryw<sid>
  key, and doRemoveXattr sends its HDEL in MULTI/EXEC (it used a plain
  pipeline under WATCH, which made the WATCH useless and hid the write from
  the read-your-writes hook).

Routing. Reads outside transactions go to the replica when it can serve them:

- doLookup (HGET entry + GET inode; the Lua lookup script stays on the master
  path because scripts are loaded on the master only), doGetAttr,
  doReadlink with noatime, doReaddir and redisDirHandler.List (HSCAN),
  fillAttr (MGET), doRead (LRANGE of chunk slices, also used by compaction,
  which re-checks the slices inside its transaction), GetXattr, ListXattr,
  doGetFacl (the inode; ACL rules are immutable and cached), getCounter for
  usedSpace and totalInodes (statfs and the background refresh).

Everything else stays on the master: every write and every read inside
txn() (WATCH/MULTI/EXEC), readlink when atime must be updated, Resolve (Lua),
doLoad, sessions, other counters, flock/plock state (coordination between
clients), doGetParents, dir stats and quotas (they feed write accounting and
quota enforcement), doList, cleanup, gc, fsck, dump and load.

Read-your-writes, with no extra round trip on the write path: a go-redis hook
on the master client adds HINCRBY ryw<sid> <field> 1 as the first command of
every MULTI/EXEC, and the client keeps the highest value returned (an atomic
max, so concurrent writers are covered). The field is random for each client
and each Reset: a sid is reused after a volume is reset or formatted again,
and a stale replica must never hold our counter from that earlier life. A
read goes to the replica only if the replica's field is at least that value;
the highest value seen on the replica is remembered, so the HGET on the
replica happens only right after a write. If the replica is behind, the read
polls it for at most one master round trip, then goes to the master. The
session's own transaction is tracked, so the writes made before it (format,
load) are ordered before any read from the replica. The key is deleted with
the session.
Plain writes outside MULTI/EXEC (counters, slice refs, dir stats, gc) are not
tracked; none of their keys is read from the replica, except usedSpace and
totalInodes, which are also updated inside transactions.

Freshness and health: every min(500ms, max-lag/4) a probe (its own
connection to the master, no retries) reads INFO replication on the master,
and a check loop reads it on the replica. The probe keeps (time,
master_repl_offset) samples, dated at half the probe's round trip since a
read from the master would not be fresher; the replica holds every write
committed before the newest sample its offset has reached. Modes:

- replica: link up, same replication id as the master, lag <= max-lag.
- master: the replica is down, not a replica, its link is down, its
  replication id differs, or its lag exceeds max-lag; reads go to the
  master. Also when the master answers with an error (ACL, ...).
- offline: the master is unreachable while the replica answers with the
  master's last replication id. Unreachable means either a dial error that
  proves it at once (connection refused, no route, network unreachable,
  name not found), or master-timeout without a single byte from the master
  on any connection (tracked by a DialHook) while the probe keeps asking.
  Timeouts alone never switch a command to offline: a slow master (a
  saturated uplink) only makes writes slow. Going offline closes the
  connections to the master, so the commands waiting for it fail with EROFS
  at once; while offline every command to the master fails with EROFS
  without being sent, and reads go to the replica however stale, since
  nothing fresher is reachable. The first answer to the probe ends it. A
  replica restarted empty, or promoted, has another replication id and is
  never served. CLIENT SETINFO is disabled on these connections: on a slow
  link each new connection cost two more round trips.

Every mode change is logged ("Read replica <addr>: mode A -> B (reason)"),
and two gauges are exported, also in the mount's .stats file:
juicefs_meta_replica_mode (-1 not checked yet, 0 master, 1 replica,
2 offline) and juicefs_meta_replica_lag_seconds (an upper bound, at the
granularity of the check interval; -1 until known).

Optimistic transactions (redis_optimistic.go): when the replica is usable,
the first attempt of txn() runs the transaction function unchanged on a
redis.Tx with a hook: WATCH/UNWATCH become no-ops, reads are served by the
replica and recorded, and MULTI/EXEC becomes one EVALSHA of a generic
script that compares each recorded read with the master's current value
and only then applies the writes with pcall (per-command errors, like
EXEC), plus the read-your-writes HINCRBY. A changed value is a conflict (TxFailedErr) and
txn() retries with WATCH on the master. Commands that cannot be checked or
replayed (SCAN, HSCAN, a write outside MULTI/EXEC such as the trash counter
or a new ACL rule, unhandled reply types, a command with more than 4000
arguments, beyond what unpack() takes in the server's Lua) abort before anything is sent and
the transaction runs with WATCH. A transaction that ends without writes
(EEXIST, ENOENT, no-op) still checks its reads in one EVALSHA, so its result
is the master's. Validating the read set at commit is serializable, and
stricter than WATCH, which does not cover reads of unwatched keys. One
generic script instead of hand-written Lua for mknod, setattr, setxattr and
write: the Go transaction functions (permissions, ACL, setgid, trash, quota
checks, SkipDirMtime, changelog) stay the only implementation, and every
transaction benefits. Integer replies of writes go through Lua numbers,
exact up to 2^53.

Round trips to the master per operation (counted at a TCP proxy, 50 ops
each), without -> with read-replica: create 5 -> 1, mkdir 5 -> 1, setattr
(times or mode) 4 -> 1, truncate 4 -> 1, setxattr 3 -> 1, removexattr
3 -> 1, write (slice commit) 4 -> 1, unlink 6 -> 1.

ACL: the client's user needs INFO on both servers, HGET on ryw* on the
replica, HINCRBY/DEL on ryw* and EVALSHA/EVAL on the master (the commands inside the script are checked
against the same user).

Tests (pkg/meta/redis_replica_test.go) run only when JFS_TEST_REPLICA_MASTER
and JFS_TEST_REPLICA point to a master and its replica: read-your-writes over
1,000 rounds with fast, slow and WAN-like links, concurrent writers, link
down, lag above max-lag, refused master (offline at once), blackholed master
(offline after master-timeout, no EROFS before), slow master (1.2 s +/- 0.6 s
for 30 s: no EROFS), optimistic conflicts and fallbacks, round trips
per operation, option errors, and the whole meta test suite with and
without read-replica.

